  <h2>What the RESO / RESC payloads actually contain</h2>
  <p>
    The <code>RESO</code> / <code>RESC</code> header is followed by an entry
    table laid out at a 0x10-byte stride: a 4-character lowercase ASCII
    fourCC tag (<code>rgcn</code>, <code>rlcn</code>, <code>rcsn</code>,
    <code>recn</code>, <code>rcan</code>, etc.), a 4-byte size, and a
    4-byte offset, with the trailing 4 bytes zeroed. Each entry points at
    a standard Nitro graphics block — <code>RGCN</code>/<code>NCGR</code>
    (character graphics), <code>RLCN</code>/<code>NCLR</code> (palette),
    <code>RCSN</code>/<code>NCSR</code> (screen / tile map),
    <code>RECN</code>/<code>NCER</code> (cell / OAM), and so on. None of
    these blocks carry human-readable text; they encode tile pixels, 5-bit
    color entries, tile indices, and OAM attribute words. The JP text
    those screens display comes from the <code>entries</code> rows of the
    relevant <code>msg*.ofs</code> files, not from the <code>.lzs</code>
    containers themselves.
  </p>

  <h2>The two text-bearing files (do not translate)</h2>
  <h3><code>inputstr/strdata.lzs</code> — kana keyboard glyph table</h3>
  <p>
    Decompressed payload is a 1,616-byte RESO container whose embedded data
    block holds the kana characters that label the buttons of the on-screen
    Japanese input keyboard. Examples: hiragana "a-column" labels
    (<code>あかさたなはまやらわ</code>) at offset <code>0x82</code>, katakana
    "a-column" (<code>アカサタナハマヤラワ</code>) at <code>0x2C2</code>, and so on.
    These are the glyphs the player taps to enter Japanese names. They are
    <strong>not translation targets</strong> — they are input alphabet data.
    Romanizing or rewriting them would break the input UI for any player who
    wants to enter a Japanese name. The English keyboard glyphs
    (<code>A-Z</code>) are part of the same file and are already present
    alongside the kana.
  </p>

  <h3><code>inputstr/taboo.bin</code> — banned-word filter</h3>
  <p>
    20,586-byte UTF-16-LE NUL-terminated string list. ~486 distinct
    kana/kanji-only entries (slang, ethnic slurs, drug terms, adult terms,
    yakuza idioms). This is the <strong>input-screen profanity filter</strong>:
    the list is consulted when the player types a custom name; if a match is
    found, the engine refuses the name. It is internal data,
    <strong>never rendered on screen</strong>, and translating it would
    weaken the filter (the JP entries catch JP slurs that an EN-only list
    would not). Leave it alone. Note the file is not LZ-wrapped —
    <code>.bin</code>, not <code>.lzs</code> — so it bypasses the
    <code>lzs.py</code> decode path.
  </p>

  <h2>False positives the strict scan filtered out</h2>
  <p>
    A permissive first-pass scan reported thousands of "JP" hits across the
    binary files. Every one investigated turned out to be a misread of
    binary structure bytes as UTF-16-LE — repeating <code>爀捧の</code> runs
    are the byte sequence <code>00 72 67 63 6E 30 ?? 00 00 ?? ?? 00 00</code>
    (trailing NUL of a size field + ASCII fourCC <code>rgcn</code> + leading
    byte of the next field) read as UTF-16-LE; <code>敃汬湁浩づ吀</code> is the
    ASCII literal <code>CellAnime0\0TXEU…</code> read as UTF-16-LE; two
    pure-CJK hits in <code>global.lzs</code> sit inside 4bpp NCGR pixel
    blobs. The strict scan (require ≥1 kana per run, 5+ kana for
    high-confidence) returns zero hits on the 19 binary files and only the
    legitimate keyboard / banned-word data on the two
    <code>inputstr/</code> files.
  </p>

